{
  "name": "Afro-Lexicon",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "createdAt": "2026-07-26",
  "purpose": "How Africans actually ask, mapped to what they actually need. The tool manifest describes 1,252 tools in their own titles — 86% carry no vocabulary beyond that — so a router built on it only understands people who already speak in tool names. This is the missing layer: pidgin and colloquial phrasing, local product and institution names, common misspellings, and the plain-English phrases people use instead of a product's title.",
  "why": "This is the part a frontier model cannot copy from the open web. Nobody outside the continent has labelled 'clear my car for tincan' as a customs-valuation intent, or 'wetin go remain' as take-home pay. Every entry added from a real user query makes routing better for the next user, which is the flywheel.",
  "growth": "Entries are seeded by hand and extended from real queries captured by the router. A phrase earns a place here when a user typed it and the router missed.",
